Maybe "they" already are coming.  This is from the Kitchener Waterloo
Record of Tuesday, July 20, 1993: 

<headline>UW to probe offensive images in computer network</headline>


by Barbar Aggerholm
Record staff

A University of Waterloo offical is looking into concerns that the
computer bulletin boards on campus are circulating violent sexual
images.  
 
As well, Matt Erikson, co-ordinator of the university's new office of
ethical behaviour and human rights, said the university is designing a
poster that will tell people how to complain if they find computer 
material offensive.

"Education is the most important thing, whether the material is
sexist, racist, or homophobic," Erickson said. 

Last week, Sonia Prevost-Derbecker, co-ordinator of the university's
Women's Centre, called on the university to stop transmission of
pornography through its computer network.

On Monday, Prevost-Derbecker said she found university newsgroups
over the weekend that included a picture of a boy engaged in oral sex
with a donkey and a woman with a dog.  There were other examples of
child pornography, she said.

Prevost-Derbecker said she will be satisfied with nothing less than
the elimination of all sex files from computer bulletin boards at the
University of Waterloo to get rid of child pornography, bestiality
and violence found on the system today.

"This is not a censorship issue," she said, "this is an issue of
harassment and human rights."

As well, "it's a totally inappropriate use of our very expensive and
oversaturated (computer) system."

UW, along with other Canadian universities, subscribes to sn
international network of more than 1,000 computers "newsgroups: or
"bulletin boards" which are divided by topic areas an include such
titles as tennis, dinosaurs and fishing. 

But there are also hundreds of files -- many from out of the country
-- featuring explicit sex, including those showing bestiality,
violence and children, Prevost-Derbecker said.  Some may originate in
Waterloo, she said.

Meanwhile, computer newsgroups are also under scrutiny at Wilfid
Laurier University, where seven sexually explicit newsgroups were
removed about a year ago, said Nora Znotinas, interim director of
computing services.

A senate committee on computing services has reviewed that decision
and has made recommendations about handling newsgroups that could go
before the Senate in September, Znotinas said.

"We're basically in the process of looking at it," she said.

Prevost-Derbecker said she wants to meet with University of Waterloo
president James Downey and the associate provost of computer services
to propose ways of dealing with computer pornography.

The only solution, she said, is to eliminate all sex newsgroups.  Even
if the newsgroup name sounds innocent, such as "Alt Sex ABC", child
pornography or violence may be hidden there, she said.

"It doesn't matter what they label the file, you can put anything in."

And if eroticism is eliminated at the same time as pornography, so be
it, Prevost-Derbecker said.  There are plenty of other places where
users can get both, she said.

"This is really an inappropriate use of our (the university's)
system...  "It doesn't belong on a system ... for scientific and
mathematical uses."

Prevost-Derbecker also wants a staff person to be assigned to each of
the university's 50 computer rooms to monitor the use of computers, as
well as to ensure users' safety.

Prevost-Derbecker praised the university's plan to develop posters
that would tell people where they can complain.  Though the university
says it has never received a formal complaint about pornographic
material, the Women's Centre has received plenty, she said.


