My apologies to Geoff Huston. The funding issue is obviously very
important. My point is that pragmatic constraints shouldn't promulgate
moralistic attitudes regarding censorship.  Why bring censorship up? There's
an inquiry happening at the moment at UNSW regarding abuse of email; it
appears that the inquiry's aims are to "make people behave more
considerately towards colleagues" [the "Weekend Australian", June
26-27], and the convenor expressed the view that the net seems to need
more laws.  There's a healthy climate for censorship at Australian
universities these days, so the conclusions of the inquiry, if it finds
that more laws are required, could be significant for the wider net community.


Put simply, if there is a problem with net traffic volumes, it ought to be 
solved without judgements as to the merit of the content of messages in a 
newsgroup; e.g. put a volume counter on net traffic, when some agreed upon 
limit is exceeded, the newsgroup gets zapped for the day. I don't know how 
possible this is.

The issue I've been addressing from the start is that there is no need
for laws regarding defamation, discrimination and harassment to be extended to
or created especially for the network.


>And who knows how much else it costs to run the individual site
>networks. Say an average of two network admins per site, that's maybe
>150k per site, just for direct staff costs.

Upkeeping the net is one of the minor tasks of a sysadmin, at least here it is.

>Greg.


