July 22 Milwaukee Sentinel
"UWM students lose work to alleged computer hacker"

By Tom Held
A computer hacker allegedly frustrated more than 60 of his fellow University
of Wisconsin - Milwaukee students by crashing a UWM computer system as the
students worked on final projects in May.

"Because of the timing, you had students up against deadlines and they could
lose hours and hours of work," said James Lowe, a UWM senior systems
programmer.  "Some did."

Lowe, who programs and maintains the computers in the Department of
Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences, said he had students pounding
on his door, asking what happened to the computers and their work.

The alleged hacker, Robert J. Keeler, 22, of 2447 N. Humboldt Ave., is a
senior studying computer science at UWM.  

Keeler has been charged with five misdemeanor counts of computer crime and
is scheduled to appear in court Friday.  He faces a maximum penalty of a
$50,000 fine and nearly four years in jail.

According to Lowe and the criminal complaint, Keeler used his home computer
to tap into UWM's "Miller" computer system, which is used primarily by
undergraduate students to do course work for computer classes.  The
undergraduate system and others at UWM are named after beer brands.

Keeler allegedly hacked his way into the program that operated the "Miller"
system and caused it to crash at least five times between May 4 and 16.  

The criminal complaint alleges that 60 to 70 students lost their work at
these times.

Lowe said the students were fine-tuning or "debugging" programs assigned as
course work.  Much of the lost work included final projects for the spring
semester, which ended May 14.

According to the complaint, Keeler told police that he learned systems
programming about a year ago and it became a challenge for him.

Lowe said the hacker had assess to the work students were doing, but he does
not believe any of the work was altered.

Tapping into the computer did not give the hacker the ability to change any
grades or alter records at UWM.

"To this day, the motivation behind this eludes me," Lowe said.  "To me, it
doesn't make a lot of sense."

Lowe said he believed the "Miller" system has been made more secure since
the incident.

School officials who operate the computer have to balance the need for
security with the desire to make the computer easy for students to use, Lowe
said.

