be construed as "an issue of harassment and human rights"?  

To the casual observer, this is gibberish.  The knowledgeable user may 
recognize this as encoded binary data, but they have no idea about the 
image represented here; it may be a nude by Degas, a NASA satellite image 
of Neptune, Miss July, a NOAA weather map, or a birth announcement for my 
new daughter.  At this stage in transmission, it's just raw data.  In 
*every* case, additional *voluntary* effort is required to view the image.  
How can we classify the mere existence of this data as either a "human 
rights issue" or harassment?  

You certainly have the authority to take action against those people who 
*USE* this material to harass others; in fact, such may be your obliga-
tion as a university official.  However, such action should be directed
against the *use* of the material, not the material itself.  

Consider a similar situation, the likes of which may have already occured
upon your campus.  This situation involves the holdings of your library.  

   "The Protocols of the Elders of Zion" is a famous piece of literature.
   It is widely regarded as fictional propaganda directed against the
   Zionist movement (and Judaism in general).  Many libraries own a copy 
   of this text; thanks to its nature and history, it has significant aca-
   demic and historic value.  If I borrow this book from the library, there 
