>newsgroups. It might have caused me to leave Cyberspace.

Come on, Carl, we know your skin is thicker than that.  If you come
into Usenet with your dermis unprotected, you're going to get hurt.
That can't be changed without adversely affecting Usenet.  Usenet
would be worthless as a medium where excessive care about politeness
and such went into every message.  One of the reasons Usenet is so
great is because you CAN dash off a note off the top of your head
without worrying about major adverse consequences.

>I've tried to remember what I've learned, but my recollection is poor.
>Here are some of the strategies I do use everyday (some of which
>probably come AT, some of which I have made up on my own):
>
>* One non-confrontational way to accept criticism X is to reply
>  "Perhaps I did X".

And if you instead wish to REJECT criticism X?

>* Try do distance the issue or problem from people. For example, don't
>  say "*your* argument is stupid", say "the argument could be
>  improved by ...."

Fine, if their argument could use improvement.  But if it is just
plain stupid, go ahead and say so.  Say what you mean.  In face to
face communication, people use weasel words like that to prevent being
accused of harshly rejecting your argument-- but their tone of voice
will indicate to the target exactly what is meant.  That technique is
low and dirty in the real world, but completely ineffective on
Usenet. Again, say what you mean.
>
>* Use phrases such as "I believe", "it seems to me", "in my opinion",
>  etc. to show that you are open to the possibility that you might be
>  wrong.

And if you aren't open to that possibility?

>* Assume the best, not the worst. When you receive criticism,
>  generally assume that it is constructive.

See?  This is why I advocate saying what you mean.  In the real world,
people will phrase nonconstructive criticism in a constructive way
because it is not acceptable to present nonconstructive criticism.  So
the target always has to try to figure out if he is being helped or
called an idiot.  If people say what they mean, there's less
ambiguity-- "You're argument is really stupid" cannot be
misinterpreted as constructive criticism.

>* When you make a mistake, admit it right away. This 1) helps everyone
>  get to the truth faster 2) gives people confidence that you can be
>  trusted not to argue for a position that you know is wrong 3) shows
>  that you are open minded 4) means that the discussion can quickly
>  move past your mistake.

Agreed, for reason 4 only.

>* Never let a public "slander" go unchallenged. To challenge a "slander"
>  minimizes the damage it causes. To let it go, maximize the damage it
>  causes.

Better: Answer slander with nastier slander of your own, and leave the
discussion once the slander made against you is forgotten.

>* Sandwich criticism between compliments.

Good for sucking up to the boss while asking for a bigger office and
more money.  Bad for Usenet.  Say what you mean.

>* It is impolite to tell people they are impolite.
So?

>* Don't reply right away. Give yourself time to cool down.

Bad idea--  gives you time to cover up your true thoughts and feelings
with a veneer of politeness.  When taking a test, the first answer
which comes to mind is usually the right answer.  Same goes for Usenet posts.

>* Before you send note, read it again.

Yes-- look for glaring factual errors and typos.  DON'T remove
inflammatory language unless it is based on a mistake you made.

>* Before you reply to a note, read it again.

Yep-- you definitely don't want to miss the word 'NOT' and look like a
complete idiot.

>* Try to end discussions on good terms with everyone, even those who
>  (you perceived as) flaming you. This usually means replying,
>  very politely, to flames.

Replying politely to flames is an interesting technique-- it's as
aggravating to the flamer as saying "Have a nice day".

>* Don't try to debate irrelevant personal insults. Either 1) ignore them or
>  2) just quote them in a reply and then ignore them.

Agreed, don't debate insults made against yourself.  If they are
really far out, ignoring them is safe.  If there is a possibility
someone might take them seriously, answer with an insult of your
own--- don't quote the insult against you.

>* Many people don't understand debating terms such as "_ad homin_",
>  "strawman", "nonsequester", "fallacy" etc. so don't use them in
>  replies. Instead, explain the fallacy from first principles or by
>  analogy.

Anyone on Usenet ought to find out what these mean.  I'll explain them
if asked, but I'm not going to give up perfectly good terms like _ad
hominem_ to the lowest common denominator.

>* Generally don't expect anyone to just take your word for something
>  because you are an authority. If you are really are an authority,
>  you should be able to make the case.

Agreed.

>* Don't expect people to do outside reading or research in support of
>  *your* argument. If some outside material is key to your argument,
>  quote it. (And then save it, so you can quote it again without
>  typing it again).

Agreed.  But DEMAND that people do the research in support of THEIR argument.

Note:  Some of the above is exaggerated and/or facetious.  But most of
it, and the general tone, is serious.  This isn't the Diogenes Club,
this is Usenet.

