Exactly right.  Different newsgroups have evolved very different codes
of "proper" behaviour.  If the newsgroup is full of people having a
good time pulling each others legs and maintaining a lot of "inside"
jokes at the expense of a poor innocent who happens to wander into it,
it's usually futile to try to fight it.  Join the fun, or decide that
group isn't your cup of tea.  The best advice is to lurk in a group a
while before posting to it to avoid nasty little foxy-paws.  A level
of flipancy which I might use in alt.folklore.urban might not be
appropriate in more "serious" groups.

What IS needed on the net is more formal support of charters for the
various groups - the FAQ's are too ad-hoc and sporadic.  It would be nice
if your reader could find and pull up reliable group charters.  A
perfect example of this is playing itself out in comp.lang.visual
right now over people using it for Visual Basic questions - with no
better named group for VB, and no way of pulling up a group charter,
