THE USE OF ELECTRONIC BULLETIN BOARDS
A STUDY OF TWO DISCOURSE COMMUNITIES







Adam C. Gross
Carnegie Mellon University
November 12, 1990
ag3j@andrew.cmu.edu




INTRODUCTION
	The ways in which people communicate are constantly changing; new 
technology brings new opportunities for personal interaction.  Within the 
past ten years, the increased use of personal computers in business and 
education has paved the way for a new medium of communication -- this 
new medium is electronic mail.  On the surface, it is the same as paper
mail, 
except that it takes literally seconds to send this mail to any properly 
connected computer in the world.  Secondly, and more importantly, the 
electronic mail (e-mail) networks allow groups of people to participate
in 
conversations on particular topics, ranging from the latest computer 
technology to the last "Twin Peaks" episode. 
	Electronic conversations, or "bulletin boards" work in a fairly 
straightforward and linear manner, where one person leaves a message 
(called "posting") and another person can respond.  Because the bboards 
operate through the use of a global computer network, people from every 
corner of the earth and from almost every profession contribute to the 
discussions.
	Differences in backgrounds of the people who use bboards  can cause 
some interesting opportunities as well as some serious problems.  For 
example, an industrial management student can participate in the same 
bboard as a corporate executive, and there is a good chance the student's 
opinions will be taken seriously.  However, this can cause complications.
 
Because the bboards are relatively new, and so many different bboards
exist, 
no formal guidelines exist on what is acceptable or appropriate behavior.
 
Added to the easy accessibility of the bboards, it is likely that people
are going 
to act inappropriately at times.  To prevent problems, some private
networks 
have already gone as far as reviewing and editing all posts before making 
them viewable.
	Educating new users on appropriate behavior might prevent some of 
these problems, but first "appropriate" behavior must be defined.  The 
purpose of my paper is to attempt to define at least part of the nature
of the 
bboards, by seeing if any distinguishable discourse communities exist. 
This 
includes determining how standards of behavior differ between bboards and 
what, if any, standards are shared.  If these differences can be
recognized, new 
users of bboards can be taught what is acceptable in each different type
of 
bboard, and potentially harmful situations can be avoided.  Learning how
to 
act is a critical step in becoming a member of a particular bboard
community, 
and because bboards are still considered somewhat experimental, if too
many 
new users fail to understand this, many bboards may reject these new
users 
entirely.  

A STUDY OF TWO BULLETIN BOARD COMMUNITIES
	I plan to analyze two discourse communities that both use the bulletin 
board system, although for different purposes.  These two communities
differ 
in a fundamental way, one is a group interested in discussing music for
fun, 
with no real academic or business purpose.  The other is a group
interested in 
discussing philosophy; their purpose is to learn about new papers on a
topic, 
to discuss existing theories and to handle some administrative purpose. 
Each 
group has a bboard on the same network dedicated to their community.  
	The data used for analysis was a selection of messages from each of the 
bboards (see appendix A).  These bboards are located on the internet
bboard 
network (of which Carnegie Mellon is a member), one of the largest bboard 
networks in the world, connecting over three million college students, 
faculty, military personal and workers at high technology firms. 
	While analyzing these messages, I attempted to find standards of 
behavior in the following categories, and to determine which standards
were 
shared among the two bboards and which were specific to each group. The 
categories are who speaks and what background is needed to speak,
accepted 
topics for discussion, seriousness and value of discussion, text features
and 
style, and the way people refer to others and interact.  Since these are
the 
characteristics that can distinguish discourse communities, they provide
a 
good means of making the comparison between the two bulletin board 
communities. 

DISCUSSION OF DIFFERENCES AND SIMILARITIES
	Although there were some interesting standards that were shared, the 
two groups that used each bboard were different enough to call them
separate 
communities. I will discuss the differences and similarities in each of
the 
areas listed below.

Who Speaks
	The community that uses the music bboard is very informal.  Anyone, 
regardless of age or academic status, is welcome to post messages and 
contribute to the conversation.  The diversity in participants is best
seen by 
looking at the list of contributors for the bboard in appendix B.  Since
each 
message has a return address, it is clear whether the speaker is
associated with 
a university, a member of the military or a worker at a company. 
Examples of 
addresses would be andrew.cmu.edu, a person at Carnegie Mellon, or 
Apple.com, a worker at Apple Computer.  
 	As there are so many people contributing, there is no one group leader 
who organizes or leads the conversations, and there tends to be a lack of 

