philosophy community's bboard is much more strict.  The members are more 
clearly defined, being composed mainly of students and faculty.  Again, 
appendix B shows a list of participants and addresses, making it is easy
to see 
that there are a limited number of people posting messages. 
Participation in 
the philosophy discussion often requires a background knowledge of
certain 
topics, such as the principles of objectivism.

Accepted Topics	
	The purpose of the music bboard is multifold and therefore broad 
range of topics are discussed. These include record reviews, concert
listings and general discussion about different topics.  Most messages
are composed of pure opinion with little support or in depth discussion. 
For example, this author actually complains about the lack of validity of
recent messages in the music bboard. 
	
"Jones! has the right to express his opinions; however it is important 
that accurate information be disseminated via the [inter]net.  This and 
other recent posting in this forum should give us all enough reason to 
second guess the opinions we hear and read." (C. Iannacone, 29 Oct 90)

This excerpt shows how varied the music bboard community members are, 
and how their standards of behavior can conflict.  
	The Philosophy bboard tends to be more focused.  Topics follow 
logically from one to the next, and posts unrelated to the topic are
fairly 
uncommon. Appendix B shows how only two different topics were discussed 
on the period from October 15 through October 19. The topic usually
relates to 
a contemporary ethical issue such as abortion or euthanasia, or a
philosophic 
analysis of a current event.  Arguments are clearly presented and backed
with 
proof from authors.  This message excerpt shows that the purpose of the 
bboard, which is narrower than the music bboard's, is to explore
philosophic 
methods and questions. 
	
"I'd like to find some sort of physical evidence to support my so-called 
logical conclusion if it contradicts something I believe is common 
knowledge." (P. Delaney, 31 Oct 90)
This sort of response to an argument is common, and it is expected that
an 
author will back his claims.

Text Features
 	Messages on the music bboard are generally short (3-10 lines of text) 
and show little planning.  One message may include several different
ideas 
and points.  Shorthand text expressions, such as "IMHO" and ";-)" are 
common.  These expressions are used to set a tone for a messages, usually 
implying that a message is of a light or humorous nature.  It is also a
sign of 
accepted informality in the community. "He sounds better, but I still
don't 
like his music much." (J. Kiparsky, 7 Oct 90)  
	The philosophy bboard's messages tend to range from from 20 to 30 
lines of text. Several page messages in organized, preplanned form are
not 
uncommon. (see P. Delaney, 21 Oct 90, Appendix A)  It is common to see a 
point by point analysis of someone else's message, including extensive
quotes 
from those messages. (J. Slenk, 1 Nov 90) The philosophy bboard has a
much 
more serious and academic tone.

Reference to Others in the Community
	There are also important differences between the two communities in 
the way the members relate to each other in the context of their
messages.  In 
the music bboard, messages range from uncaring to outright rude, often 
childish, attacks on previous authors.

"Here we get a guy so completely ignorant of the music of the past three 
decades that he's never ever *heard* of funk, but he's still soooo 
goddamn smart that he can proclaim from his lofty perch that 'RAP 
ISN'T MUSIC.'" (R. Shea, 31 Oct 90)
In this example, the author is referring to a previous author's
criticism.  This 
rude approach does little for his argument, and shows the type of
relationship 
between member of the music community.  By contrast, the philosophy 
community shows a professional respect for each other.
	
"But assuming you accept my definitions, and I assume even Mr. Rapier 
accepts the same properties that I do concerning numbers, addition, and 
equality.  So I'll try to prove that things from that, by introducing new 
definitions and then mixing and matching." (P. Delaney, 21 Oct 90)
This sample is an example of how common it is to see direct references to
a 
previous speaker and his ideas.  It is more important to keep an academic 
relationship because their are fewer members participating in the
philosophy 
bboard, else the whole community's methods and productivity could be 
ruined by a argument between two members.

SUMMARY OF RESULTS
	This comparison of the two discourse communities suggests that there 
are differences in standards of behavior in a number of areas. 
Essentially, the 
authors, topics, features and relationships of members of the two bboards 
were entirely different.  These differences are related to the purpose of
each 
community and the specialization or generality of its audience.  The
music 
bboard was a very casual and laid back community, while the philosophy 
bboard engaged in serious conversation with a focused purpose, the
learning 
of philosophy.
	The differences in the areas of text features shows that certain bboards 
impose much more strict requirements on who can and cannot post 
messages.  At the same time, both bboards recognize certain tones in a 
message, tones in speech used merely to incite an argument, often called
a 
"flame." (R. Elinson, 5 Oct 90)  
	This suggests that new users learning to use the system are always 
welcome to read most bboards, though becoming a participating member in a 
specialized community is another issue.  If a user wants to participate
as a 
member of a specialized community, he must first look to see what type of 
requirements are placed one posting.  If a post on a bboard has an
informal 
tone, the response is usually informal as well, placing less demand on
the 
responder.  The new user can freely post in such situations, but if the
bboard 
is more focused and academic, the user should be sure he understands the 
material being discussed, or else he may be humiliated in front of the
entire 
community he is trying to address.
	New users must also learn what type of behavior is never considered 
appropriate or welcome, regardless of the community.  This includes being
a 
"flame" or interacting rudely and disrespectfully with other members of
the 
community.  Entering some bboard communities is like jumping into the 
middle of a conversation, so the user has to understand that he will be 
responsible for what is said.  Any personal attacks are almost
universally 
considered in bad taste and inappropriate.
	Hopefully, given a background in bboard standards of behavior, new 
users will be able to quickly become productive members of the
communities 
they choose to join.  What is most important to remember is that
standards of 
behavior, on the whole, differ from community to community. Users should 
be able to spot these differences and react accordingly, although shared 
characteristics which basically reflect common sense, such as not being 
deliberately obnoxious, are also found.  If new users do not learn how to
act 
properly, no one will know how to effectively use the new medium of 
electronic bboards, and this medium which has a great potential to bring 
different people and ideas together, could be wasted.

