	At MIT, for a time an Associate Provost took part in a local
electronic discussion group devoted these issues. As a sample of his
views, I quote from an article he wrote to a newsletter on our system
"... the norms of our community have not yet equated the leaving of an image
such as I have described above in a public place for anyone to view with
other acts of violence such as striking someone with one's fist. I
believe the equation is justified and neither act of violence should be
countenanced." (Athena Insider, January 1991).
	The moment he started to post, every participating
flamer^H^H^H^H^Hskilled electronic debater engaged him in a debate about
the topic 1/2 :-). I have never seen such a unity among this group of
individuals, who usually spent their time ripping each other's arguments
(in other discussion areas) to pieces. The forum being local and the
posters all somewhat experienced, the discussion was reasonable by
net.standards (no *senseless* insults, no _ad hominem_ attacks, no
juvenile misogyny). But the experience of being opposed by many forceful
individuals was undoubtably unpleasant (to be charitable), and I suspect
it was very unfamiliar to him as well. He didn't remain a participant
for long (I briefly asked him about it some months later, he said
basically it was taking up too much of his attention).
	The usual arrangement is that people in power pontificate in a
setup that admits no rebuttal. For all the talk of "dialogue" or
"community discussion", it is an extraordinarily rare occasion when one
can pin down a member of an administration for an extended public
discussion. And rarer still for it to be in the context where assertions
can be extensively analyzed, and any evasions challenged or publically
exposed (e.g. "The Supreme Court has ruled 'hostile environments' to be
illegal" [go check cases, read original text] "Wait a minute, that
involves extreme conduct 'severe and pervasive', nothing at all like 'if
someone feels harassed, then they have been'").
	I conjecture that anyone with some influence will reason that it
is simply a waste of their time to debate on the net. Their effort is
much better spent lobbying the significant people and committees for the
policies they wish imposed. Perhaps this is a lesson with some relevance
to opponents of restrictive policies. We needn't give up the USENET we
hold so dear :-), but doing some of the arguing to the world at large
might be a good idea.

