                          PRESS RELEASE

              Ordeal Continues for Author and Scholar
        Researcher Rik Scarce Subjected to Punitive Conditions

~Date:     30 June 1993    
Contact:  Bob Greene      Beth Frey
	      509-332-4279    509-334-7831

    PULLMAN, WA -- Author and researcher Rik Scarce has been a prisoner in
the Spokane County Jail since May 14.  Scarce, a doctoral candidate in
sociology at Washington State University, was jailed for refusing to answer
certain questions before a federal grand jury investigating a raid on a WSU
lab in 1991, an incident members of the Animal Liberation Front have taken
credit for.  Scarce himself is not charged with any crime and is not a
suspect in the raid.  He does not support those involved in the raid or their
actions.  Yet he remains in jail, his imprisonment the result of the grand
jury's broad powers of "coercive" detention.  

    As a scholar and researcher, who wrote about the ALF in his book,
Eco-Warriors: Understanding the Radical Environmental Movement, Rik has
consistently refused to answer questions which he says may compromise pledges
of confidentiality given to research subjects.  In doing so, Rik stands
firmly on the principles of academic freedom and the inviolability of
confidential sources.  These are principles recognized by the American
Sociological Society (ASA), which has expressed concern over his case, as
well as the Office of Research Grant and Research Development (OGRD) at WSU,
which operates under federal regulations related to the protection of human
research subjects.  Rik's stand has received support from the WSU Graduate
and Professional Students Association and the Washington State ACLU.  He has
received favorable coverage in The Chronicle for Higher Education and the
Moscow Pullman Daily News.  Sadly, this support has not prevented abominable
treatment at the hands of the federal prosecutor, two county jails and the
unelected, unaccountable grand jury.

    The Daily News, in an editorial April 10, 1993 that supported Scarce,
said, "The grand jury is the tool of choice for prosecutors who want to
badger innocent people until they are willing to swear to anything...  .
This inquisition is tantamount to a license to destroy academic (more)
freedom...  ." Now that Rik is in jail, the inquisition has begun in earnest.

    On June 12, Rik was temporarily moved from the Spokane County Jail to the
Franklin County Jail in Pasco, WA as the result of a strike by Spokane County
employees.  Quoted in the Daily News as being "disturbed and curious" at
being one of the first federal prisoners moved to a different jail, out of
more than a hundred incarcerated in Spokane, his situation soon became more
disturbing.  After being taken to Pasco in handcuffs and manacles, Scarce
was, while in Franklin County:

	--Denied Visitation Rights
	--Refused Mail
	--Forced to Sleep on the Floor of an Overcrowded Cell
	--Denied Reading Material

Additionally, before and during his imprisonment Rik and his wife have
incurred vast legal costs, as well as travel expenses between Pullman and
Spokane and loss of income.  Again, all of this has happened to a man accused
of no crime.

    As difficult as the ordeal of this man has been so far, it is far from
over.  Rik may be held until the grand jury's term expires in December, or
until a federal judge decides his treatment is punitive rather than coercive.
For this to happen, Rik urgently needs broad-based media coverage of his
situation, with in-depth examination of the injustice being committed against
him.  Beyond that, the interests of every freedom-loving American demand
intense media scrutiny of the grand jury system, and the enormous potential
for abuse that is an inherent part of that system.

    Individuals concerned about academic freedom and federal abuse of power
against innocent people are urged to write or call the U.S.  Attorney's
Office in Spokane, WA (509-353-2767) and write Janet Reno, Attorney General
of the United States, asking that Rik Scarce be released from jail.
Contributions are also urgently needed for the Rik Scarce Legal Defense Fund,
PO Box 2463 CS, Pullman, WA 99165, to cover Rik's mounting legal fees and
planned appeal to the U.S.  Supreme Court.

