Excuse me, but when did I say we should censor ourselves? I certainly
never censor myself; I just try to behave the way I want other people 
to behave towards me. 

I have defied the law when it needed defying. I have stood up in open court
and told a judge he could send me to jail because I would not, on principle,
pay a fine. If, in the same vein, those people who dismiss the child 
pormography laws want to stand up, announce what they plan to do, and defy
the authorities to arrest them, then I disagree with them, but I don't believe
such actions will harm the net. But posting (often anonymously) material that
will put the system administrators who run the servers it lands on in legal
peril does not defy an unjust law; it merely shifts the risks to the
administrators and the net as a whole.

Also, I will confess, I do see a contradiction here. The practise of posting
(at least on a.b.p.e) as though nobody ever heard of a concept called copyright
reflects a contempt for the very concept of creator's rights that helped make
the software that drives the net possible in the first place. The posting of
material that promotes violence (including child sexual abuse) also violates
the civilised basis of the net. 

In conclusion, I would say that if we insist on confusing the anarchy that
prevails on the net with lawlessness, then whether our current freedom goes
out with a bang or a whimper, it will go out.

