George Michaelson offers the prototype of the typical argument against 
a Bill of Rights.  I'll offer a counterargument against each of his
points in turn.


	Why should the net be any different to other modes of expression, upon
	which curtailment of absolute rights of freedom seems to be common,
	(and not just volountary) and also backed up by penalty?



In the United States, Canada and France, the Right to Freedom of
Expression is protected by law in the constitution. A Right is by
definition "absolute" (see the recent judgement in a US Court on "hate
speech"); after it is guaranteed in law, the question transfers to how best
to limit the dangers of it.   Through the accumulation of precedents,
especially in recent years,   the Right to Freedom of expression 
is increasingly gaining the status of _fact_ in those countries. The
absolute right is not as absent as George thinks it is. Let's say that
that's a fact.

I would have hoped that a man in the George's academic position
would agree that a knowledge of basic history does not depend on the place
in which one was educated; instead, he reminds us that he was educated
in Scotland. Is this his excuse for ignoring American and French history?


        John [McCarthy] knows I am not an Australian. My Edinburgh upbringing 
	and parental influences do not tend to motivate me to defending 
	fascist and racist rights to free speech.

Let's not dwell on George's apparent ignorance of the difference between
hate speech and speech that is about to cause danger, as defined in
American law. 

Unable to find motivation to defend fascist and racist rights to free
speech, George, instead, does not mind asserting the right to speak
whatever views he deems appropriate, subject to the bureaucratic constraints 
of decorum inside his head, of course. If a racist is polite, is it OK
if he tells his views in public?  Just who are the racists, George? Have
you been reading the recent news on Mabo?

The curtailments to free speech, on the other hand, are indeed common.
In Australia, a career politician of integrity can be sacked from his
position for expressing the wrong opinion (it happened a few days ago); 
likewise an academic. There is an atmosphere of intolerence in Australia 
promulgated *especially* by those who share George's following opinion: 

	On another note I can stand up for most of Carl Adies statement on
	rights of expression, but half of that equation is missing here (in my
	institution) being:

        	An attempt to teach the basis of reasonable behaviour.


It's a very British thing to do, as we know.   


	I've tried very hard to see why its in the interests of the owners of
	the network (I'm not one) to accept an assertion of free speech
	rights.  Short of an acceptance of volontary self-censorship by
	posters, I can't see it - the risks to fragile funding by external
	bodies that take this right less seriously is too great.

I'm wondering what exactly are the objections George is raising here. If
he is afraid of public dissatisfaction on moral grounds leading to curtailment 
of network funds, then a Bill of Rights clears that up - there'd be no
legal way the two could be linked. 

There are good arguments against voluntary self-censorship. The position
implies the existence of a moralizing authority, a keeper of society's
morals, for there's always something that offends somebody, and at some
stage the authority will have to decide what's reasonable and what's
not.   They will have to defend themselves against interest groups, etc.
At the moment, it's the sysadmins in the front line, then
ultimately the University Senate - and we've already seen why it is
undesirable for the Senate to play this role.  

A guaranteed freedom of expression is firstly in the interests of the 
individual staff members and public, then for the institution of
learning since it allows a free discussion of ideas. Even racist comments 
contribute to debate.  Isn't "discussion of ideas" what university is
supposed to be about?  Spurious racist slurs are uninteresting;
premeditated ones can precipitate fruitful debate - self-censorship
doesn't even figure in the consideration. 

I believe there's a good case that the reason why the Courts and the
Kennetts can get away with what they are doing with Mabo is to do with the
word "racist" having such a dirty mystique about it: the British inheritance
stifles open debate.  It tries to constrain unpalatable things through
silence and ignoring them; unfortunately, it's easy in a legalistic
system such as ours to consign them under a different guise - easy in
the sense that suddenly you gain respectability from the populous doing it. 
Snake-oil salesmen do a roaring trade in parliament.


	When the network becomes something which motivates quality staff to
	stay or go, perhaps the establishment will act out of self-interest as
	research and teaching staff migrate to institutions with more
	freedoms.

This begs the question: with a Bill of Rights, nobody could be sacked on
the grounds of simply offensive speech.

Second, it's facile to suggest that some kind of war of attrition would
occur between university authorities and staff for the right of free speech. 
The self-interest should be obvious from thinking about the value of
open discussion of ideas, the ideal that no longer applies to Australian
universities. 

The statement could have come from someone who has little appreciation
of the benefit of talking freely or disscussing ideas freely. Are you
