If someone is serious about producing "rules" of proper conduct, they
should also describe the range of actual practice -- in many newsgroups,
even so-called "serious" ones, this bears little resemblance to Kadie's
stated rules.  Newcomers who are unaware of the difference between
"proper" and "actual" practice will fall easy prey to the miscreants who
seem to be found everywhere.

Newcomers should also be warned that the identification of miscreants
is often difficult.  One practice common in many newsgroups is to
simply ignore the miscreants.  Unfortunately, the silence that results
from shunning is indistinguishable from the silence that signifies
agreement.  Little better is the practice of "respectfully dis-
agreeing".  Consequently, it can take a long time to determine who
are the miscreants, and whether they are representative of the group
or the exception.

Illustrating the above point, I was surprised to see this posting by
Carl Kadie taken seriously by so many.  The portrait that Kadie presents
of himself and of the rules he follows are in stark contrast to my
experience with him.  Although I largely agree with the rules as being
desirable behavior, I cannot help but see Kadie's advocacy of them as
an elaborate joke.

In my "interaction" with Kadie, he violated many of the rules he
described.  Although I have seen many others whose conduct was much
worse than his, his was bad enough to earn Kadie my continuing
animosity.  This is just another example of how failure to follow
the "rules" can earn you enemies.  But my experience is also an example
(again, quite common) of the risks of following the "rules" (of pausing
before responding and of trying to be careful, accurate and detached
in one's messages):  I took a severe beating at the hands of Kadie and
company.  However, this was a choice that I made (lesser evil), knowing
the likely consequences.  Imagine now much angrier a newcomer would be,
not knowing that he or she had made such a choice.

Note: I am not a regular reader of this group -- I check it only
when I know of events affecting friends and colleagues that might
result in their becoming the subject of discussions here.

Douglas B. Moran
moran@ai.sri.com


