An admin at NCSU is quoted by sebridge@eos.ncsu.edu says:

[...]
>-Using the internet for personal communication is supposed to be limited to
>-those messages which are associated with scholarly activity.
[...]

This is not NSFnet policy, it says:

"SPECIFICALLY ACCEPTABLE USES:
[...]
(3) Communication and exchange for professional development, to
    maintain currency, or to debate issues in a field or subfield of
    knowledge.
[...]
(9) Communication incidental to otherwise acceptable use, except for
    illegal or specifically unacceptable use.
[...]

UNACCEPTABLE USES:
[...]
(11) Extensive use for private or personal business."
     ********** (emphasis added)

So what is academic use? The "Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms
of Students", the main statement on academic freedom for students in
the U.S.  begins:

  "Academic institutions exist for the transmission of knowledge, the
   pursuit of truth, the development of students, and the general
   well-being of society. Free inquiry and free expression are
   indispensable to the attainment of these goals. As members of the
   academic community, students should be encouraged to develop the
   capacity for critical judgment and to engage in a sustained and
   independent search for truth."

As for laws prohibiting the University from competing with private
business, in my state this law is not a blanket prohibition. If it
was:
  The university library would have to stop carrying videotapes, because
  this competes with video rental stores.

  The university bookstore would have to stop selling books, because
  this competes with private bookstores.

  The university itself would have to stop offering education, because
  this competes with private universities.

Unless North Carolina has suddenly gone Libertarian, I doubt is the
admin's reasons are based on sound legal advice.

The world of the net is becoming increasingly important for general
scholarly activity. It would be a shame if NCSU decided to put
unnecessary barriers between its academic community and this new
world. In any case, it NCSU does decide cloister its academic
community, the decision should be made based on sound legal advice and
with due deliberation by, for example, the university senate.

- Carl
