Some of her solutions lack realism (at Ontario's minimum wage, her
proposal to staff all the 50 computer rooms at Waterloo would cost
1.5 million dollars a year). And I suspect she has a limited technical
understanding of the net. But...

> What mystfies me, is that people with decision making authority
> continue to listen to lunitics like this, which somehow (wrongfully)
> gives credance to the political agendas that these creeps bring with them.

People share these concers. They don't just represent a tiny minority.
The vast majority of Canadians, for example, detest child pornography.
If indeed she found a copy of a picture of a person under 18 having 
sex with an animal on a public site at Waterloo, I'd suggest most Canadians
would probably agree with her that this represents a problem.

Of course she has an agenda. And I suspect that most of her concern
relates to far less egregious abuses than the really serious porn that
gets posted. But it does get posted. After the descriptions I have 
seen on a.b.p.e, I can't dismiss the possibility that somebody did
post child pornography to a public bbs/newsgroup at Waterloo. 

> The more bizarre the suggestions the far left make in their efforts to
> 'social engineer' us into oblivion, the less outragious their demands appear 
> to become. Their continual frontal assault on institutions like, freedom of
> speech, freedom of thought, etc. creates a 'hostile environment' for people 
> who resist. The BORG (Star Trek - Next Generation) could do well to learn from
> her group. After all, their slogans appear to be the same, "Resistance is
> futile". Its obvious that Prevost-Derbecker is a very frustrated person. 

The social engineer has no greater ally than the irresponsible citizen.
For the "amiable anarchy" of the net to continue, we must act with at 
least a minimum of responsibility to each other and to the society
around us. Not everybody shares that sense of responsibility. The
Alt.erotica group often gets pictures posted that violate the laws on
child protection in many jurisdictions, including Canada. People who 
point out that this exposes people such as system administrators to
risks (imprisonment, infamy, and a criminal record come to mind) they
never consented to take simply get dismissed as "hysteroids". For some
people, apparently, the freedom of the net amounts to complete license.

In their minds, the temporary immunity the network provides from laws
on copyright and child pornography gets equated with genuine freedom.
Well, we see the natural result in Ms. Prevost-Derbecker's case. She
would not have the ammunition to make the assault on the net she does
if someone had not provided it by (apparently) posting a highly
objectionable picture. 

